== 2(a) the hazard a floor implies. A rival's odds against the default under fixed share: O -> (1-h)O + h r, for small O (r the rival's share of a redraw). So the odds cannot fall below about h r: a soft floor at log2(h r) bits. A floor at 0 with threshold theta counters of u bits each: the distance from floor to the MAP's boundary is theta u = log2(1 / (h r)), so h r = 2^(-theta u). u = log2((1-e)/e) if the counter's unit is one hit at lie rate e.
   e | u bits | h r at theta 2 (1/0) | h r at theta 3 (sequences) | total hazard a taste (1/0 + 5 sequences) | mean scored tastes between changes | seconds at 0.63 tastes a s
   0.235 | 1.70 | 0.09437 | 0.02899 | 0.23931 | 4.2 | 6.6
   0.2 | 2.00 | 0.06250 | 0.01562 | 0.14062 | 7.1 | 11.3
   0.1 | 3.17 | 0.01235 | 0.00137 | 0.01920 | 52.1 | 82.7
   0.05 | 4.25 | 0.00277 | 0.00015 | 0.00350 | 285.8 | 453.6
   0.025 | 5.29 | 0.00066 | 0.00002 | 0.00074 | 1348.2 | 2139.9
   So the setting of the hazard depends on which lie rate a counter's unit is read at; at the measured +1 of about 1.7 bits (D31), e .235, a total hazard of 0.24 a taste. No single h matches a counter across worlds whose lie rates differ, since a counter's step doesn't change with e, while a Bayesian's does.

